. Bottom-left panel:  \n   Header: “Critical Limitation”  \n   Text: “Leads to inefficient routing under real conditions.”\n\n4. Bottom-right panel:  \n   Header: “Key Insight”  \n   Text: “Wind can be utilized to improve efficiency.”\n\nThe overall design uses a dark background with high-contrast white and blue text for readability. The layout visually emphasizes the central problem (“Problems”) and its four contributing factors, suggesting a conceptual framework for addressing wind-related challenges in urban unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) and unmanned aircraft system (UAM) operations. The infographic is likely intended for technical or operational audiences in the aerospace or autonomous systems field.</p>\n  </figcaption>"}], "categories": ["paragraph", "figure"], "total_char_count": 1737, "line_count": 21, "has_visual": true, "visual_type": ["figure"], "alignment": null, "text_sparse": false, "image_only": false, "raw_text": "| Wind is notjust disturbance |\nProblem Statement\n![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">infographic,diagram,flowchart</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">This is a structured infographic diagram centered around the theme “Problems,” displayed in bold white text within a large blue circular node at the center. The circle contains a white line-art icon depicting two documents — one with a bar chart and the other with a graph — symbolizing data analysis or path planning. Radiating outward from the center are four rectangular black panels, each connected by thin gray lines, arranged in a cross layout.\n\nEach panel contains white text with a blue header, presenting a key problem or insight:\n\n1. Top-left panel:  \n   Header: “Wind Significantly Affects UAV Performance”  \n   Text: “Urban UAV/UAM operations are strongly influenced by wind.”\n\n2. Top-right panel:  \n   Header: “Conventional Path Planning”  \n   Text: “Conventional path planning focuses on shortest distance.”\n\n3. Bottom-left panel:  \n   Header: “Critical Limitation”  \n   Text: “Leads to inefficient routing under real conditions.”\n\n4. Bottom-right panel:  \n   Header: “Key Insight”  \n   Text: “Wind can be utilized to improve efficiency.”\n\nThe overall design uses a dark background with high-contrast white and blue text for readability. The layout visually emphasizes the central problem (“Problems”) and its four contributing factors, suggesting a conceptual framework for addressing wind-related challenges in urban unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) and unmanned aircraft system (UAM) operations. The infographic is likely intended for technical or operational audiences in the aerospace or autonomous systems field.</p>\n  </figcaption>"}, {"slide_no": 3, "title": "I Wind is not just disturbance |", "blocks": [{"category": "heading1", "text": "I Wind is not just disturbance |"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "RESEARCH OBJECTIVE"}, {"category": "figure", "text": "![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,infographic,map,schematic</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">This is a stylized, isometric 3D diagram of an urban environment designed for drone navigation, presented as an infographic map. The scene depicts a dense cityscape with white and teal 3D buildings arranged in a grid, set against a dark blue background. A yellow dashed line traces a path from a green “START” marker at the top center to a red “GOAL” marker at the bottom center, indicating a flight route. The path is annotated with key navigation concepts: “NAGIVING ALLEYWAYS,” “TIGHT CANYON TRAVERSAL (High Energy),” and “SHORTEST PATH.” \n\nVisual cues include blue arrows indicating wind direction and energy flow, hexagonal vents on building rooftops, and a small drone icon along the path. Two data panels overlay the map: one on the left labeled “High Energy Expenditure: Tightly Configured Torn” and another on the right labeled “Lower Energy/ Tailwind Efficiency: Open Street Current.” The right side also features a section titled “WIND-OPTIMIZED EFFICIENT PATH” with a green arrow and wind swirls. The overall design suggests a simulation or game interface for optimizing drone flight paths through urban terrain, emphasizing energy efficiency, wind currents, and path optimization. All text is in a futuristic, sans-serif font with color-coded emphasis (green for start/goal, yellow for path, blue for wind/energy).</p>\n  </figcaption>"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "Wind"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "$ Minimizing\nCost"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Develop a wind-aware path\nplanning framework"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Evaluate using cost-based metrics:"}, {"category": "list", "text": "● Path length\n● Wind interaction (headwind/tailwind)\n● Total cost"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Comparison"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Compare three strategies:"}, {"category": "list", "text": "● Shortest path\n● Wind avoidance\n● Wind utilization"}], "categories": ["heading1", "paragraph", "figure", "list"], "total_char_count": 1791, "line_count": 22, "has_visual": true, "visual_type": ["figure"], "alignment": null, "text_sparse": false, "image_only": false, "raw_text": "I Wind is not just disturbance |\nRESEARCH OBJECTIVE\n![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,infographic,map,schematic</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">This is a stylized, isometric 3D diagram of an urban environment designed for drone navigation, presented as an infographic map. The scene depicts a dense cityscape with white and teal 3D buildings arranged in a grid, set against a dark blue background. A yellow dashed line traces a path from a green “START” marker at the top center to a red “GOAL” marker at the bottom center, indicating a flight route. The path is annotated with key navigation concepts: “NAGIVING ALLEYWAYS,” “TIGHT CANYON TRAVERSAL (High Energy),” and “SHORTEST PATH.” \n\nVisual cues include blue arrows indicating wind direction and energy flow, hexagonal vents on building rooftops, and a small drone icon along the path. Two data panels overlay the map: one on the left labeled “High Energy Expenditure: Tightly Configured Torn” and another on the right labeled “Lower Energy/ Tailwind Efficiency: Open Street Current.” The right side also features a section titled “WIND-OPTIMIZED EFFICIENT PATH” with a green arrow and wind swirls. The overall design suggests a simulation or game interface for optimizing drone flight paths through urban terrain, emphasizing energy efficiency, wind currents, and path optimization. All text is in a futuristic, sans-serif font with color-coded emphasis (green for start/goal, yellow for path, blue for wind/energy).</p>\n  </figcaption>\nWind\n$ Minimizing\nCost\nDevelop a wind-aware path\nplanning framework\nEvaluate using cost-based metrics:\n● Path length\n● Wind interaction (headwind/tailwind)\n● Total cost\nComparison\nCompare three strategies:\n● Shortest path\n● Wind avoidance\n● Wind utilization"}, {"slide_no": 4, "title": "I Wind is not just disturbance |", "blocks": [{"category": "heading1", "text": "I Wind is not just disturbance |"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "MODELING APPROACH & ASSUMPTION"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Wind Field Wix,y)"}, {"category": "figure", "text": "![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,infographic,heatmap,schematic</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">This image is a schematic diagram overlaid with a color-coded heatmap, likely representing a system of interconnected components or data flow. The background features a gradient of blue to yellow-green, with white directional arrows indicating flow or interaction paths. Seven dark gray rectangular blocks, labeled B1 through B7, are arranged in a loose grid pattern. Each block is surrounded by glowing yellow-orange regions, suggesting high activity or intensity at those points. White arrows connect the blocks, implying relationships or data movement between them. The labels “B1” through “B7” are clearly visible in white text within each respective block. The overall visual style suggests a technical or scientific visualization, possibly from a simulation, network analysis, or computational model. The color gradient and arrow overlays imply a dynamic or probabilistic system, with intensity or value represented by the color scale.</p>\n  </figcaption>"}, {"category": "figure", "text": "![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,3D grid,technical illustration,coordinate system visualization</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">A 3D isometric diagram of a rectangular grid plane, rendered in light blue with dashed lines forming a uniform square mesh, set against a black background. The grid is tilted to appear three-dimensional, with perspective lines suggesting depth. Two labeled axes are visible: “Along X” on the right side and “Along Y” on the left side, both written in white sans-serif font. The entire scene is enclosed within a thin, glowing cyan border. The image serves as a technical illustration of a 2D plane with defined spatial axes, likely used in computer graphics, CAD, or physics simulations to represent coordinate systems or spatial grids.</p>\n  </figcaption>"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Urban environment modeled as a 2D grid"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "● We use a grid to make path planning simple and easy to\ncompute, while still keeping the overall city structure."}, {"category": "figure", "text": "![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,scientific visualization,heat map,simulation output,technical illustration</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">This image displays a scientific visualization, likely from a computational fluid dynamics (CFD) or electromagnetic simulation, showing a color-coded field distribution—possibly electric, magnetic, or pressure—over a 2D domain. The background consists of a gradient color map ranging from deep blue (low intensity) to bright yellow and red (high intensity), with contour lines indicating field strength or gradient. Two rectangular regions, possibly representing physical components or sensor placements, are overlaid on the field. These regions contain internal grid patterns, suggesting they are areas of interest for measurement or analysis. The entire visualization is framed by a dark border with a light blue rounded inner edge, indicating it may be a screenshot or output from a simulation software interface. No text or numerical labels are visible within the image, but the structure implies quantitative data representation for engineering or physics analysis.</p>\n  </figcaption>"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Wind field defined as spatial function W(x,y)"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "● We model wind as a function of position to show how wind\nchanges across space, without using complex simulations."}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "Assumptions"}, {"category": "list", "text": "● 2D planar motion (fixed altitude)\n● Constant UAV speed relative to wind"}, {"category": "list", "text": "● No dynamic constraints (point-mass model)\n● Static and fully known wind field\n● No uncertainty or disturbances beyond wind"}, {"category": "figure", "text": "![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,3D render,technical illustration,product visualization</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">A 3D computer-generated diagram of a white, multi-rotor aerial vehicle, likely a personal or urban air mobility (UAM) aircraft, presented against a solid black background. The vehicle features a sleek, aerodynamic fuselage with a dark blue or black canopy or cockpit area. It is equipped with four rotors — two mounted on each of the two main wings — suggesting a fixed-wing or hybrid design for vertical takeoff and landing (VTOL) capability. The wings are swept back and extend horizontally, with visible landing gear or skids beneath. The entire model is enclosed within a rounded square frame with a light blue border, indicating it may be a thumbnail, icon, or promotional graphic. No text, labels, or scale indicators are visible. The image serves as a technical or conceptual visualization of a future-style drone or eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing) aircraft.</p>\n  </figcaption>"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "UAM modeled as point-mass\nwith heading direction"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "● We simplify the UAM to focus on how flight direction interacts\nwith wind, without adding complex flight dynamics."}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Cost depends on both position(x,y) and motion direction(theta)"}], "categories": ["heading1", "paragraph", "figure", "list"], "total_char_count": 5024, "line_count": 40, "has_visual": true, "visual_type": ["figure"], "alignment": null, "text_sparse": false, "image_only": false, "raw_text": "I Wind is not just disturbance |\nMODELING APPROACH & ASSUMPTION\nWind Field Wix,y)\n![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,infographic,heatmap,schematic</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">This image is a schematic diagram overlaid with a color-coded heatmap, likely representing a system of interconnected components or data flow. The background features a gradient of blue to yellow-green, with white directional arrows indicating flow or interaction paths. Seven dark gray rectangular blocks, labeled B1 through B7, are arranged in a loose grid pattern. Each block is surrounded by glowing yellow-orange regions, suggesting high activity or intensity at those points. White arrows connect the blocks, implying relationships or data movement between them. The labels “B1” through “B7” are clearly visible in white text within each respective block. The overall visual style suggests a technical or scientific visualization, possibly from a simulation, network analysis, or computational model. The color gradient and arrow overlays imply a dynamic or probabilistic system, with intensity or value represented by the color scale.</p>\n  </figcaption>\n![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,3D grid,technical illustration,coordinate system visualization</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">A 3D isometric diagram of a rectangular grid plane, rendered in light blue with dashed lines forming a uniform square mesh, set against a black background. The grid is tilted to appear three-dimensional, with perspective lines suggesting depth. Two labeled axes are visible: “Along X” on the right side and “Along Y” on the left side, both written in white sans-serif font. The entire scene is enclosed within a thin, glowing cyan border. The image serves as a technical illustration of a 2D plane with defined spatial axes, likely used in computer graphics, CAD, or physics simulations to represent coordinate systems or spatial grids.</p>\n  </figcaption>\nUrban environment modeled as a 2D grid\n● We use a grid to make path planning simple and easy to\ncompute, while still keeping the overall city structure.\n![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,scientific visualization,heat map,simulation output,technical illustration</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">This image displays a scientific visualization, likely from a computational fluid dynamics (CFD) or electromagnetic simulation, showing a color-coded field distribution—possibly electric, magnetic, or pressure—over a 2D domain. The background consists of a gradient color map ranging from deep blue (low intensity) to bright yellow and red (high intensity), with contour lines indicating field strength or gradient. Two rectangular regions, possibly representing physical components or sensor placements, are overlaid on the field. These regions contain internal grid patterns, suggesting they are areas of interest for measurement or analysis. The entire visualization is framed by a dark border with a light blue rounded inner edge, indicating it may be a screenshot or output from a simulation software interface. No text or numerical labels are visible within the image, but the structure implies quantitative data representation for engineering or physics analysis.</p>\n  </figcaption>\nWind field defined as spatial function W(x,y)\n● We model wind as a function of position to show how wind\nchanges across space, without using complex simulations.\nAssumptions\n● 2D planar motion (fixed altitude)\n● Constant UAV speed relative to wind\n● No dynamic constraints (point-mass model)\n● Static and fully known wind field\n● No uncertainty or disturbances beyond wind\n![image](/image/placeholder)\n\n  <figcaption>\n    <p class=\"figure-type\">diagram,3D render,technical illustration,product visualization</p>\n    <p class=\"figure-description\">A 3D computer-generated diagram of a white, multi-rotor aerial vehicle, likely a personal or urban air mobility (UAM) aircraft, presented against a solid black background. The vehicle features a sleek, aerodynamic fuselage with a dark blue or black canopy or cockpit area. It is equipped with four rotors — two mounted on each of the two main wings — suggesting a fixed-wing or hybrid design for vertical takeoff and landing (VTOL) capability. The wings are swept back and extend horizontally, with visible landing gear or skids beneath. The entire model is enclosed within a rounded square frame with a light blue border, indicating it may be a thumbnail, icon, or promotional graphic. No text, labels, or scale indicators are visible. The image serves as a technical or conceptual visualization of a future-style drone or eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing) aircraft.</p>\n  </figcaption>\nUAM modeled as point-mass\nwith heading direction\n● We simplify the UAM to focus on how flight direction interacts\nwith wind, without adding complex flight dynamics.\nCost depends on both position(x,y) and motion direction(theta)"}, {"slide_no": 5, "title": "| Wind is notjust disturbance |", "blocks": [{"category": "heading1", "text": "| Wind is notjust disturbance |"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "OUR FIRST APPROAH"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Wind cost depends on direction\nnot only the position (x,y)"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Wind effect changes with flight direction"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Same location can be headwind or tailwind"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "C(x,y,)"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "·"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "·"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Find a path that minimizes\nthe total accumulated cost"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "J = Spath C(x,y,) ds"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "ds: small segment of the path"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Route with minimum total cost"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Total path cost is computed by\nintegrating along all possible paths"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "x, y: position\n⌀: heading direction"}], "categories": ["heading1", "paragraph"], "total_char_count": 430, "line_count": 18, "has_visual": false, "visual_type": [], "alignment": null, "text_sparse": false, "image_only": false, "raw_text": "| Wind is notjust disturbance |\nOUR FIRST APPROAH\nWind cost depends on direction\nnot only the position (x,y)\nWind effect changes with flight direction\nSame location can be headwind or tailwind\nC(x,y,)\n·\n·\nFind a path that minimizes\nthe total accumulated cost\nJ = Spath C(x,y,) ds\nds: small segment of the path\nRoute with minimum total cost\nTotal path cost is computed by\nintegrating along all possible paths\nx, y: position\n⌀: heading direction"}, {"slide_no": 6, "title": "IA* algorithm |", "blocks": [{"category": "heading1", "text": "IA* algorithm |"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "WHAT IS A* ALGORITHM?"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "Problem we want to solve"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "When a UAM travels from the\ndeparture point S to the destination G,"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Shorter table time is better"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Lower fuel/energy consumption is better"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "A* algorithm with\ncomplex cost function"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Ci = Wtti + Wei + Wri"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Route with minimum total cost"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Lower risk is better"}, {"category": "figure", "text": "![image](/image/placeholder)"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Adjustable weights"}], "categories": ["heading1", "paragraph", "figure"], "total_char_count": 349, "line_count": 14, "has_visual": true, "visual_type": ["figure"], "alignment": null, "text_sparse": false, "image_only": false, "raw_text": "IA* algorithm |\nWHAT IS A* ALGORITHM?\nProblem we want to solve\nWhen a UAM travels from the\ndeparture point S to the destination G,\nShorter table time is better\nLower fuel/energy consumption is better\nA* algorithm with\ncomplex cost function\nCi = Wtti + Wei + Wri\nRoute with minimum total cost\nLower risk is better\n![image](/image/placeholder)\nAdjustable weights"}, {"slide_no": 7, "title": "IA* algorithm |", "blocks": [{"category": "heading1", "text": "IA* algorithm |"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "HOW A* WORKS"}, {"category": "caption", "text": "Find a optimized route from start node to goal node"}, {"category": "chart", "text": "![image](/image/placeholder)\n- Grid Chart\n- A grid-based chart displaying numerical values in cells, with some cells highlighted in green and blue, and one cell in red. Each cell contains a number and a small circle connected by a line, suggesting a relationship or mapping between the number and the circle. The highlighted cells include a green square with the value 50 and a blue square with the value 00, both centered in their respective cells.\n| 64 | 50 | 44 |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 14 | 50 | 10 | 30 |\n| 70 | 50 | 00 | 50 |\n| 84 | 70 | 60 | 64 |"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "Main Idea"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "a plane is discretized into a grid, and the final path is constructed\nby connecting small line segments from node to node."}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "How do we determine the next node?"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "f(n) = g(n) + h(n)"}, {"category": "list", "text": "● g(n): actual cost from the start node to node n\n● h(n): heuristic estimate from node nto the goal\n● f(n): estimated total cost of the path through node n"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "Between 9 nodes, select node with lowest f(n)"}], "categories": ["heading1", "paragraph", "caption", "chart", "list"], "total_char_count": 1022, "line_count": 20, "has_visual": true, "visual_type": ["chart"], "alignment": null, "text_sparse": false, "image_only": false, "raw_text": "IA* algorithm |\nHOW A* WORKS\nFind a optimized route from start node to goal node\n![image](/image/placeholder)\n- Grid Chart\n- A grid-based chart displaying numerical values in cells, with some cells highlighted in green and blue, and one cell in red. Each cell contains a number and a small circle connected by a line, suggesting a relationship or mapping between the number and the circle. The highlighted cells include a green square with the value 50 and a blue square with the value 00, both centered in their respective cells.\n| 64 | 50 | 44 |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 14 | 50 | 10 | 30 |\n| 70 | 50 | 00 | 50 |\n| 84 | 70 | 60 | 64 |\nMain Idea\na plane is discretized into a grid, and the final path is constructed\nby connecting small line segments from node to node.\nHow do we determine the next node?\nf(n) = g(n) + h(n)\n● g(n): actual cost from the start node to node n\n● h(n): heuristic estimate from node nto the goal\n● f(n): estimated total cost of the path through node n\nBetween 9 nodes, select node with lowest f(n)"}, {"slide_no": 8, "title": "IA* algorithm |", "blocks": [{"category": "heading1", "text": "IA* algorithm |"}, {"category": "paragraph", "text": "HOW A* WORKS"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "Find a optimized route from start node to goal node"}, {"category": "table", "text": "|  |  | 72 42 30 | 58 38 20 | 52 42 10 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 58 28 30 | 44 24 20 | 38 28 10 | 38 38 00 |\n| 64 14 50 | 50 10 40 | 44 14 30 | 44 24 20 | 52 42 10 |\n| 70 10 60 | 50 00 50 | 50 10 40 | 58 28 30 |  |\n| 84 14 70 | 70 10 60 | 64 14 50 |  |  |"}, {"category": "list", "text": "This case, lets use\n- Euclidean distance for g(n) f(n) = g(n) 十 h(n)\n- Menhaten distance for h(n)"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "Update Rule"}, {"category": "heading1", "text": "For each neighbor:"}, {"category": "list", "text": "- calculate new g(n)\n- estimate h(n)\n- compute f(n)=g(n)+h(n)\n- update the path if the new cost is smaller"}], "categories": ["heading1", "paragraph", "table", "list"], "total_char_count": 578, "line_count": 18, "has_visual": true, "visual_type": ["table"], "alignment": null, "text_sparse": false, "image_only": false, "raw_text": "IA* algorithm |\nHOW A* WORKS\nFind a optimized route from start node to goal node\n|  |  | 72 42 30 | 58 38 20 | 52 42 10 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 58 28 30 | 44 24 20 | 38 28 10 | 38 38 00 |\n| 64 14 50 | 50 10 40 | 44 14 30 | 44 24 20 | 52 42 10 |\n| 70 10 60 | 50 00 50 | 50 10 40 | 58 28 30 |  |\n| 84 14 70 | 70 10 60 | 64 14 50 |  |  |\nThis case, lets use\n- Euclidean distance for g(n) f(n) = g(n) 十 h(n)\n- Menhaten distance for h(n)\nUpdate Rule\nFor each neighbor:\n- calculate new g(n)\n- estimate h(n)\n- compute f(n)=g(n)+h(n)\n- update the path if the new cost is smaller"}, {"slide_no": 9, "title": "| A* algorithm |", "blocks": [{"category": "heading1", "text": "| A* algorithm |"}, {"category": "parag